ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Convert to String (Brute Force)

Where it comes from: Common beginner approach found in coding discussions.

Idea: Convert the number to a string of text. Walk through every character, turn it back into a digit, and check if it divides the number.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> selfDividingNumbers(int left, int right) {
        vector<int> ans;
        for (int i = left; i <= right; i++) {
            string s = to_string(i);
            bool valid = true;
            for (char ch : s) {
                int digit = ch - '0';
                if (digit == 0 || i % digit != 0) {
                    valid = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (valid) {
                ans.push_back(i);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(N log10(R)) to build the string and scan characters. Space is O(log10(R)) extra space to hold the string.

What makes the next approach better: Converting an integer to a string uses unnecessary memory. We can extract digits using basic math instead.

Approach 2: Direct Modulo and Division (Your Approach - Optimal)

Where it comes from: Classic textbook digit extraction.

Idea: Keep a copy of the number. Extract the last digit with modulo 10, check divisibility against the original number, and drop the last digit with division by 10 until nothing remains.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    bool checkDivide(int n) {
        int temp = n;
        while (temp > 0) {
            int digit = temp % 10;
            if (digit == 0 || n % digit != 0) {
                return false;
            }
            temp /= 10;
        }
        return true;
    }

public:
    vector<int> selfDividingNumbers(int left, int right) {
        vector<int> ans;
        for (int i = left; i <= right; i++) {
            if (checkDivide(i)) {
                ans.push_back(i);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(N log10(R)) since it performs one iteration per digit. Space is O(1) auxiliary space using purely scalar variables.

What makes the next approach better: Your approach is already the best one. We can only make tiny visual cleanups, like using compound assignment operators.

